   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "You are a new supplier. How do we know the skid will pass installation and operational qualification (IQ/OQ)?",
     "a": "Your quality unit approves the FAT protocol. You witness a wet FAT, in person at [test bay location] or over [remote witness platform], and review the executed report and turnover package before the skid ships. FAT proves function on our test water; SAT and OQ prove performance on your feed. FAT deviations close in our shop."
    },
    {
     "q": "Are the skids registered for pressure and the panels certified?",
     "a": "Pressure-retaining parts are registered under CSA B51 with a Canadian registration number (CRN) for your province [confirm who registers and for which provinces]. Control panels are built and marked to [panel standard and certification mark held]."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you handle cold feed water in winter?",
     "a": "RO output falls as water cools. An array rated at 25 °C can lose a third or more of its output at 5 °C, and many Canadian supplies reach that in winter. We size at your lowest recorded feed temperature, or add a heat exchanger, and show capacity at both ends of your range in the mass balance."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our municipal supply is chloraminated. What changes?",
     "a": "Polyamide RO membranes do not tolerate chloramine, so it is removed first, which releases ammonia. RO rejects ammonium well, but raising pH to control CO₂ turns more of it into dissolved ammonia gas, which passes the membrane to the EDI. So we design chloramine removal and the CO₂ strategy together. Pretreatment covers the removal step."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our feed comes from a private well. Is that a problem?",
     "a": "Not in itself. USP and Ph. Eur. require PW to be made from water of drinking water quality, so you need to show your well meets it. Iron, manganese, hardness and silica then drive the pretreatment, and we ask for analyses from more than one season because well chemistry moves."
    },
    {
     "q": "Hot water or chemical sanitization?",
     "a": "Hot water at 80 °C or more leaves no residual to rinse out and test for, and the cycle is proven by temperature at the coldest point. Chemical sanitization suits membranes not rated for heat, but adds a residual test to every cycle. Frequency is set from your trend data during performance qualification (PQ)."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who services the skid after handover?",
     "a": "Our service team, from [service locations]. Membranes, EDI modules and seal kits for your skid are stocked in [stocking location], and a service plan gives you a stated on-site response for a severity 1 fault of [severity 1 on-site response time, hours]. Every visit ends with a signed report your quality unit can file."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do you build hospital, laboratory or industrial water systems?",
     "a": "The same RO and EDI platform is used for hospital reprocessing water (CSA Z314), laboratory reagent water (ASTM D1193) and industrial demineralized water. Our scope there: [hospital, lab and industrial water scope]. This page describes the pharmaceutical build."
    }
